/T (EIGRP's composite metric uses bandwidth and delay \(and optionally reliability/load\). Therefore, it can distinguish a high-bandwidth path from a lower-bandwidth path when the interfaces are configured with correct bandwidth and delay values. IS-IS also uses configurable link metrics; a suitable metric design can distinguish the paths. The exact EIGRP metric requires the configured delay and bandwidth values, which are not provided in the question, so a numerical EIGRP metric cannot be calculated from link speeds alone. OSPF is still a good choice when its reference bandwidth is set correctly.)
